1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,911
Imagine opening your Grade Twelve
Mathematics Paper One and meeting

2
00:00:04,911 --> 00:00:09,822
this equation: two multiplied by two to
the power of

3
00:00:09,822 --> 00:00:14,734
two x, minus nine multiplied by two to
the power

4
00:00:14,734 --> 00:00:18,714
of x, plus four, equals zero.

5
00:00:18,714 --> 00:00:21,927
The x is in the exponent.

6
00:00:21,927 --> 00:00:27,104
The first power looks twice as large as
the second.

7
00:00:27,104 --> 00:00:29,826
And there are three terms.

8
00:00:29,826 --> 00:00:33,315
Where would you even begin?

9
00:00:33,315 --> 00:00:38,226
Here is the surprise: this is actually a
quadratic equation

10
00:00:38,226 --> 00:00:39,474
in disguise.

11
00:00:39,474 --> 00:00:43,178
And it is not a made-up challenge.

12
00:00:43,178 --> 00:00:48,089
It is Question one point one point four
from the

13
00:00:48,089 --> 00:00:52,591
November twenty twenty-five National
Senior Certificate Mathematics Paper One,

14
00:00:52,591 --> 00:00:55,098
worth four marks.

15
00:00:55,098 --> 00:00:58,311
Welcome back to Equation Station SA.

16
00:00:58,311 --> 00:00:59,559
I'm Chris.

17
00:00:59,559 --> 00:01:04,470
In Lesson Five, we are going to uncover
exponential equations

18
00:01:04,470 --> 00:01:09,382
and hidden quadratics, build the method
step by step, and

19
00:01:09,382 --> 00:01:12,594
practise the mistakes examiners really
see.

20
00:01:12,594 --> 00:01:17,506
By the end, you should be able to
recognise the

21
00:01:17,506 --> 00:01:22,959
pattern before anyone tells you which
method to use.

22
00:01:22,959 --> 00:01:25,681
Think back to Lesson One.

23
00:01:25,681 --> 00:01:30,592
If I show you x squared minus five x plus

24
00:01:30,592 --> 00:01:35,503
six equals zero, you can factorise it
into x minus

25
00:01:35,503 --> 00:01:39,698
two, multiplied by x minus three, equals
zero.

26
00:01:39,698 --> 00:01:42,911
The answers are two and three.

27
00:01:42,911 --> 00:01:46,615
We know how to solve that quadratic.

28
00:01:46,615 --> 00:01:51,526
Our job today is to recognise when the
same structure

29
00:01:51,526 --> 00:01:55,997
is hiding inside a very different-looking
equation.

30
00:01:55,997 --> 00:01:59,210
First, know where the unknown sits.

31
00:01:59,210 --> 00:02:04,121
In x squared, x is the base, and two is

32
00:02:04,121 --> 00:02:05,370
the exponent.

33
00:02:05,370 --> 00:02:10,281
In two to the power of x, two is the

34
00:02:10,281 --> 00:02:13,494
base, and x is the exponent.

35
00:02:13,494 --> 00:02:16,215
That second expression is exponential.

36
00:02:16,215 --> 00:02:21,127
A negative value of x is allowed; for
example, two

37
00:02:21,127 --> 00:02:26,580
to the power of negative one is one half.

38
00:02:26,580 --> 00:02:31,491
We only need a few exponent ideas to
unlock today's

39
00:02:31,491 --> 00:02:32,249
questions.

40
00:02:32,249 --> 00:02:37,160
When the bases are the same, multiplying
powers means adding

41
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:38,408
their exponents.

42
00:02:38,408 --> 00:02:43,319
When a power is raised to another power,
multiply the

43
00:02:43,319 --> 00:02:44,076
exponents.

44
00:02:44,076 --> 00:02:48,988
And a positive base other than one gives
a unique

45
00:02:48,988 --> 00:02:52,477
output for each real exponent.

46
00:02:52,477 --> 00:02:57,388
Here is a quick example from the kind of
practice

47
00:02:57,388 --> 00:02:59,127
on our website.

48
00:02:59,127 --> 00:03:04,120
Three to the power of the bracket x minus
one,

49
00:03:04,120 --> 00:03:05,369
equals twenty-seven.

50
00:03:05,369 --> 00:03:07,599
Twenty-seven is three cubed.

51
00:03:07,599 --> 00:03:12,510
So three to the power of x minus one
equals

52
00:03:12,510 --> 00:03:13,759
three cubed.

53
00:03:13,759 --> 00:03:18,936
The bases match, which means x minus one
equals three.

54
00:03:18,936 --> 00:03:21,934
Therefore x equals four.

55
00:03:21,934 --> 00:03:24,656
That is the same-base method.

56
00:03:24,656 --> 00:03:29,567
One exponential expression equals
another, and we can make the

57
00:03:29,567 --> 00:03:31,582
bases identical.

58
00:03:31,582 --> 00:03:34,795
What if the bases are different?

59
00:03:34,795 --> 00:03:39,707
Consider eight to the power of x equals
four to

60
00:03:39,707 --> 00:03:43,902
the power of the bracket x plus one.

61
00:03:43,902 --> 00:03:48,097
Eight is two cubed; four is two squared.

62
00:03:48,097 --> 00:03:53,008
So the left becomes two to the power of
three

63
00:03:53,008 --> 00:03:53,765
x.

64
00:03:53,765 --> 00:03:58,676
The right becomes two to the power of two
times

65
00:03:58,676 --> 00:04:01,398
the bracket x plus one.

66
00:04:01,398 --> 00:04:05,593
Carefully expand that exponent: two x
plus two.

67
00:04:05,593 --> 00:04:10,504
Now three x equals two x plus two, giving
x

68
00:04:10,504 --> 00:04:11,752
equals two.

69
00:04:11,752 --> 00:04:16,664
A quick check confirms that eight squared
and four cubed

70
00:04:16,664 --> 00:04:19,170
are both sixty-four.

71
00:04:19,170 --> 00:04:23,365
But please do not use that method
blindly.

72
00:04:23,365 --> 00:04:28,277
We could equate those exponents because
two exponential expressions were

73
00:04:28,277 --> 00:04:29,034
equal.

74
00:04:29,034 --> 00:04:33,945
We cannot just equate exponents across
terms joined by plus

75
00:04:33,945 --> 00:04:36,452
and minus signs.

76
00:04:36,452 --> 00:04:40,156
Look at the original matric question
again.

77
00:04:40,156 --> 00:04:42,386
It has three terms.

78
00:04:42,386 --> 00:04:47,297
What we need now is the single most
important identity

79
00:04:47,297 --> 00:04:49,804
in this lesson.

80
00:04:49,804 --> 00:04:54,716
Two to the power of two x is the square

81
00:04:54,716 --> 00:04:58,419
of two to the power of x.

82
00:04:58,419 --> 00:05:00,650
Let that sink in.

83
00:05:00,650 --> 00:05:05,561
Two to the power of two x equals the
bracket

84
00:05:05,561 --> 00:05:10,524
two to the power of x, all squared.

85
00:05:10,524 --> 00:05:15,435
That does not mean two multiplied by two
to the

86
00:05:15,435 --> 00:05:17,174
power of x.

87
00:05:17,174 --> 00:05:20,878
We can check with x equals two.

88
00:05:20,878 --> 00:05:25,073
Two to the power of four equals sixteen.

89
00:05:25,073 --> 00:05:28,777
Two squared, all squared, also equals
sixteen.

90
00:05:28,777 --> 00:05:32,481
But two times two squared is eight.

91
00:05:32,481 --> 00:05:35,970
The last expression is different.

92
00:05:35,970 --> 00:05:39,183
Why does the correct rule work?

93
00:05:39,183 --> 00:05:43,378
Because two x means x multiplied by two.

94
00:05:43,378 --> 00:05:48,289
When we square the entire power two to
the power

95
00:05:48,289 --> 00:05:53,466
of x, the power-of-a-power rule
multiplies its exponent by two.

96
00:05:53,466 --> 00:05:58,377
So the result is two to the power of two

97
00:05:58,377 --> 00:05:59,902
x.

98
00:05:59,902 --> 00:06:04,588
Now look closely at what repeats in our
question.

99
00:06:04,588 --> 00:06:09,499
The first term contains the square of two
to the

100
00:06:09,499 --> 00:06:11,239
power of x.

101
00:06:11,239 --> 00:06:16,150
The second term contains two to the power
of x

102
00:06:16,150 --> 00:06:17,889
on its own.

103
00:06:17,889 --> 00:06:22,883
This is the same pattern as two times
something squared,

104
00:06:22,883 --> 00:06:27,354
minus nine times that something, plus
four.

105
00:06:27,354 --> 00:06:31,058
Let's give that something a new name.

106
00:06:31,058 --> 00:06:35,744
Let k equal two to the power of x.

107
00:06:35,744 --> 00:06:40,655
Then two to the power of two x becomes k

108
00:06:40,655 --> 00:06:42,180
squared.

109
00:06:42,180 --> 00:06:47,091
Our entire equation transforms into two k
squared minus nine

110
00:06:47,091 --> 00:06:49,813
k plus four equals zero.

111
00:06:49,813 --> 00:06:54,990
We have turned an exponential equation
into an ordinary quadratic.

112
00:06:54,990 --> 00:06:59,901
The letter k is only a temporary
substitute; our final

113
00:06:59,901 --> 00:07:03,881
job is still to find x.

114
00:07:03,881 --> 00:07:08,792
Before solving, one useful observation: k
must be greater than

115
00:07:08,792 --> 00:07:13,970
zero, because two to any real power is
always positive.

116
00:07:13,970 --> 00:07:18,881
Keep that in mind when you later decide
which answers

117
00:07:18,881 --> 00:07:20,897
are possible.

118
00:07:20,897 --> 00:07:23,618
Now try the factorisation yourself.

119
00:07:23,618 --> 00:07:26,831
Pause if you need a moment.

120
00:07:26,831 --> 00:07:31,742
Which two brackets will give two k
squared minus nine

121
00:07:31,742 --> 00:07:34,249
k plus four?

122
00:07:34,249 --> 00:07:39,160
We get two k minus one, multiplied by k
minus

123
00:07:39,160 --> 00:07:40,900
four, equals zero.

124
00:07:40,900 --> 00:07:45,811
Multiply the brackets to check: two k
squared, minus eight

125
00:07:45,811 --> 00:07:48,533
k, minus k, plus four.

126
00:07:48,533 --> 00:07:53,444
That is exactly two k squared minus nine
k plus

127
00:07:53,444 --> 00:07:54,968
four.

128
00:07:54,968 --> 00:07:59,961
By the zero-product property, two k minus
one equals zero,

129
00:07:59,961 --> 00:08:03,174
or k minus four equals zero.

130
00:08:03,174 --> 00:08:08,628
So k equals one half, or k equals four.

131
00:08:08,628 --> 00:08:10,367
Stop right here.

132
00:08:10,367 --> 00:08:13,089
Are those our final answers?

133
00:08:13,089 --> 00:08:13,846
No.

134
00:08:13,846 --> 00:08:18,041
The original question asks for x, not k.

135
00:08:18,041 --> 00:08:22,952
This is the step many candidates failed
to complete, according

136
00:08:22,952 --> 00:08:27,863
to the Department of Basic Education's
twenty twenty-five Mathematics Diagnostic

137
00:08:27,863 --> 00:08:29,388
Report.

138
00:08:29,388 --> 00:08:32,601
Return to the meaning of k.

139
00:08:32,601 --> 00:08:38,545
We said k equals two to the power of x.

140
00:08:38,545 --> 00:08:43,457
For the first branch, two to the power of
x

141
00:08:43,457 --> 00:08:45,196
equals one half.

142
00:08:45,196 --> 00:08:50,373
One half is two to the power of negative
one.

143
00:08:50,373 --> 00:08:53,862
So x equals negative one.

144
00:08:53,862 --> 00:08:58,774
For the second branch, two to the power
of x

145
00:08:58,774 --> 00:09:00,022
equals four.

146
00:09:00,022 --> 00:09:02,252
Four is two squared.

147
00:09:02,252 --> 00:09:05,250
So x equals two.

148
00:09:05,250 --> 00:09:10,162
The two answers are x equals negative
one, or x

149
00:09:10,162 --> 00:09:12,177
equals two.

150
00:09:12,177 --> 00:09:16,863
Notice that negative one is a perfectly
valid exponent.

151
00:09:16,863 --> 00:09:21,549
We did not say x had to be positive.

152
00:09:21,549 --> 00:09:26,461
We said two to the power of x had to

153
00:09:26,461 --> 00:09:27,709
be positive.

154
00:09:27,709 --> 00:09:32,180
Those statements are not the same thing.

155
00:09:32,180 --> 00:09:36,375
Let's verify both answers using the
original question.

156
00:09:36,375 --> 00:09:41,287
For x equals negative one, the first term
is two

157
00:09:41,287 --> 00:09:44,499
times one quarter, or one half.

158
00:09:44,499 --> 00:09:49,411
The second term is nine times one half,
or four

159
00:09:49,411 --> 00:09:51,150
and a half.

160
00:09:51,150 --> 00:09:56,061
One half minus four and a half plus four
equals

161
00:09:56,061 --> 00:09:57,586
zero.

162
00:09:57,586 --> 00:10:02,497
For x equals two, the first term is two
times

163
00:10:02,497 --> 00:10:04,236
sixteen, or thirty-two.

164
00:10:04,236 --> 00:10:08,923
The middle term is nine times four, or
thirty-six.

165
00:10:08,923 --> 00:10:13,885
Thirty-two minus thirty-six plus four
also equals zero.

166
00:10:13,885 --> 00:10:15,624
Both solutions work.

167
00:10:15,624 --> 00:10:20,536
The original question was worth four
marks, and the important

168
00:10:20,536 --> 00:10:25,222
lesson is not just the final pair of
numbers.

169
00:10:25,222 --> 00:10:30,215
It is the chain of reasoning: spot a
repeated expression,

170
00:10:30,215 --> 00:10:35,208
rewrite its square, substitute, solve the
quadratic, return to x,

171
00:10:35,208 --> 00:10:37,224
and check.

172
00:10:37,224 --> 00:10:42,217
Let's give that routine a short name:
recognise, substitute, solve,

173
00:10:42,217 --> 00:10:43,465
return, verify.

174
00:10:43,465 --> 00:10:48,376
And remember, sometimes you must rewrite
the bases even before

175
00:10:48,376 --> 00:10:51,865
you can recognise the substitution.

176
00:10:51,865 --> 00:10:56,776
Now I want to test whether you can carry
that

177
00:10:56,776 --> 00:11:01,688
logic into a question where the two bases
do not

178
00:11:01,688 --> 00:11:03,427
appear to match.

179
00:11:03,427 --> 00:11:08,338
This is where strong exam technique
begins: not memorising one

180
00:11:08,338 --> 00:11:13,250
exact question, but understanding why the
method works when the

181
00:11:13,250 --> 00:11:15,265
numbers change.

182
00:11:15,265 --> 00:11:20,176
Look at four to the power of x, minus
five

183
00:11:20,176 --> 00:11:25,088
times two to the power of x, plus four,
equals

184
00:11:25,088 --> 00:11:26,612
zero.

185
00:11:26,612 --> 00:11:31,523
We have a four in one base and a two

186
00:11:31,523 --> 00:11:33,263
in the other.

187
00:11:33,263 --> 00:11:35,985
But four is two squared.

188
00:11:35,985 --> 00:11:40,896
That means four to the power of x is two

189
00:11:40,896 --> 00:11:45,807
to the power of two x, which is the
square

190
00:11:45,807 --> 00:11:49,511
of two to the power of x.

191
00:11:49,511 --> 00:11:53,491
The repeated expression is visible again.

192
00:11:53,491 --> 00:11:58,177
Let k equal two to the power of x.

193
00:11:58,177 --> 00:12:03,088
The equation becomes k squared minus five
k plus four

194
00:12:03,088 --> 00:12:04,337
equals zero.

195
00:12:04,337 --> 00:12:09,330
Factorise into k minus one, multiplied by
k minus four,

196
00:12:09,330 --> 00:12:10,578
equals zero.

197
00:12:10,578 --> 00:12:15,540
So k equals one or k equals four.

198
00:12:15,540 --> 00:12:17,771
Translate both answers back.

199
00:12:17,771 --> 00:12:22,682
Two to the power of x equals one gives x

200
00:12:22,682 --> 00:12:23,931
equals zero.

201
00:12:23,931 --> 00:12:28,842
Two to the power of x equals four gives x

202
00:12:28,842 --> 00:12:30,090
equals two.

203
00:12:30,090 --> 00:12:33,303
The solutions are zero and two.

204
00:12:33,303 --> 00:12:38,214
Check the first in the original: one
minus five plus

205
00:12:38,214 --> 00:12:39,953
four equals zero.

206
00:12:39,953 --> 00:12:45,898
Check the second: sixteen minus twenty
plus four equals zero.

207
00:12:45,898 --> 00:12:50,093
What made this different from our exam
question?

208
00:12:50,093 --> 00:12:51,833
Only the appearance.

209
00:12:51,833 --> 00:12:56,744
We had to rewrite four to the power of x

210
00:12:56,744 --> 00:12:59,957
before the repeated expression became
obvious.

211
00:12:59,957 --> 00:13:03,937
The underlying method stayed the same.

212
00:13:03,937 --> 00:13:09,114
Now comes the trap I really want you to
understand.

213
00:13:09,114 --> 00:13:14,025
Consider four to the power of x, plus
three times

214
00:13:14,025 --> 00:13:19,970
two to the power of x, minus four, equals
zero.

215
00:13:19,970 --> 00:13:24,656
Try the substitution before listening to
the next step.

216
00:13:24,656 --> 00:13:29,342
Let k equal two to the power of x.

217
00:13:29,342 --> 00:13:34,253
We obtain k squared plus three k minus
four equals

218
00:13:34,253 --> 00:13:35,011
zero.

219
00:13:35,011 --> 00:13:40,004
Factorise into k plus four, multiplied by
k minus one,

220
00:13:40,004 --> 00:13:42,019
equals zero.

221
00:13:42,019 --> 00:13:46,931
The quadratic says k equals negative
four, or k equals

222
00:13:46,931 --> 00:13:47,688
one.

223
00:13:47,688 --> 00:13:53,632
Is every quadratic answer valid in the
original exponential equation?

224
00:13:53,632 --> 00:13:58,544
No. k equals two to the power of x, and

225
00:13:58,544 --> 00:14:03,455
two to the power of x is always strictly
positive

226
00:14:03,455 --> 00:14:05,194
for real x.

227
00:14:05,194 --> 00:14:07,916
It cannot equal negative four.

228
00:14:07,916 --> 00:14:10,423
Reject that branch.

229
00:14:10,423 --> 00:14:13,144
The other branch is possible.

230
00:14:13,144 --> 00:14:18,056
Two to the power of x equals one, so x

231
00:14:18,056 --> 00:14:19,304
equals zero.

232
00:14:19,304 --> 00:14:23,284
This is our only real solution.

233
00:14:23,284 --> 00:14:26,497
Be precise about what we rejected.

234
00:14:26,497 --> 00:14:30,201
We rejected negative k, not negative x.

235
00:14:30,201 --> 00:14:35,112
In the official exam question, x equals
negative one was

236
00:14:35,112 --> 00:14:40,023
allowed because two to the power of
negative one is

237
00:14:40,023 --> 00:14:41,763
positive one half.

238
00:14:41,763 --> 00:14:46,674
Here, negative four is proposed as the
output of two

239
00:14:46,674 --> 00:14:52,127
to the power of x, and that is
impossible.

240
00:14:52,127 --> 00:14:57,039
Picture the graph of y equals two to the
power

241
00:14:57,039 --> 00:14:58,287
of x.

242
00:14:58,287 --> 00:15:01,500
It stays above the horizontal axis.

243
00:15:01,500 --> 00:15:06,411
Even when x becomes very negative, the
graph approaches zero

244
00:15:06,411 --> 00:15:08,641
but never reaches it.

245
00:15:08,641 --> 00:15:14,095
That is why k cannot be negative or zero.

246
00:15:14,095 --> 00:15:16,817
A brief comparison will help.

247
00:15:16,817 --> 00:15:21,728
If k equals x squared, then k may be zero

248
00:15:21,728 --> 00:15:22,976
or positive.

249
00:15:22,976 --> 00:15:27,887
If k equals two to the power of x, k

250
00:15:27,887 --> 00:15:30,118
must be strictly positive.

251
00:15:30,118 --> 00:15:35,029
We always inherit the restrictions of the
original expression we

252
00:15:35,029 --> 00:15:36,554
replaced.

253
00:15:36,554 --> 00:15:41,465
Now, how do we choose a method quickly in
an

254
00:15:41,465 --> 00:15:42,222
examination?

255
00:15:42,222 --> 00:15:44,452
Start with two questions.

256
00:15:44,452 --> 00:15:49,364
First: is one exponential expression
equal to another, and can

257
00:15:49,364 --> 00:15:52,577
I give them the same base?

258
00:15:52,577 --> 00:15:55,789
If yes, consider equating the exponents.

259
00:15:55,789 --> 00:16:00,701
Second: do I see the same expression
appearing once and

260
00:16:00,701 --> 00:16:04,404
squared in an equation with several
terms?

261
00:16:04,404 --> 00:16:07,402
If yes, consider substitution.

262
00:16:07,402 --> 00:16:12,314
For instance, three to the power of the
bracket x

263
00:16:12,314 --> 00:16:16,509
plus one equals eighty-one is a same-base
question.

264
00:16:16,509 --> 00:16:21,420
Eighty-one equals three to the fourth, so
x plus one

265
00:16:21,420 --> 00:16:22,668
equals four.

266
00:16:22,668 --> 00:16:25,666
Therefore x equals three.

267
00:16:25,666 --> 00:16:30,577
But three to the power of two x, minus
ten

268
00:16:30,577 --> 00:16:35,488
times three to the power of x, plus nine,
equals

269
00:16:35,488 --> 00:16:38,210
zero, is a hidden quadratic.

270
00:16:38,210 --> 00:16:43,121
If k equals three to the power of x, the

271
00:16:43,121 --> 00:16:48,033
equation becomes k squared minus ten k
plus nine equals

272
00:16:48,033 --> 00:16:48,790
zero.

273
00:16:48,790 --> 00:16:53,967
Its factors are k minus one and k minus
nine.

274
00:16:53,967 --> 00:16:58,878
Hence k equals one or nine, giving x
equals zero

275
00:16:58,878 --> 00:17:00,894
or two.

276
00:17:00,894 --> 00:17:05,089
You don't have to memorise two unrelated
tricks.

277
00:17:05,089 --> 00:17:10,266
The first equation is solved by making
matching powers equal.

278
00:17:10,266 --> 00:17:15,177
The second uses a repeated expression and
the quadratic structure

279
00:17:15,177 --> 00:17:17,684
you already know.

280
00:17:17,684 --> 00:17:21,388
Let's remove some of my guidance now.

281
00:17:21,388 --> 00:17:26,299
On the screen is two to the power of two

282
00:17:26,299 --> 00:17:31,292
x, minus six times two to the power of x,

283
00:17:31,292 --> 00:17:33,523
plus eight, equals zero.

284
00:17:33,523 --> 00:17:35,262
Pause the video.

285
00:17:35,262 --> 00:17:40,174
Write the substitution, find every
possible k, and return to

286
00:17:40,174 --> 00:17:43,663
x before checking my answer.

287
00:17:43,663 --> 00:17:45,893
Here is the solution.

288
00:17:45,893 --> 00:17:50,579
Let k equal two to the power of x.

289
00:17:50,579 --> 00:17:55,757
Then k squared minus six k plus eight
equals zero.

290
00:17:55,757 --> 00:18:00,934
We factorise into k minus two, times k
minus four.

291
00:18:00,934 --> 00:18:04,147
So k equals two or four.

292
00:18:04,147 --> 00:18:09,058
Returning to the original variable gives
x equals one or

293
00:18:09,058 --> 00:18:09,815
two.

294
00:18:09,815 --> 00:18:15,269
Both corresponding k-values are positive,
so both are possible.

295
00:18:15,269 --> 00:18:20,180
If you stopped at k equals two and k
equals

296
00:18:20,180 --> 00:18:25,091
four, now is the time to repair that
habit. k

297
00:18:25,091 --> 00:18:27,322
is the temporary variable.

298
00:18:27,322 --> 00:18:30,319
The question wants x.

299
00:18:30,319 --> 00:18:35,231
We can take today's idea further without
adding a brand-new

300
00:18:35,231 --> 00:18:36,479
solving technique.

301
00:18:36,479 --> 00:18:41,165
A hidden quadratic does not have to be
exponential.

302
00:18:41,165 --> 00:18:46,158
Look at x to the fourth, minus thirteen x
squared,

303
00:18:46,158 --> 00:18:49,156
plus thirty-six, equals zero.

304
00:18:49,156 --> 00:18:52,369
It looks like a fourth-degree equation.

305
00:18:52,369 --> 00:18:57,280
But x to the fourth is the square of x

306
00:18:57,280 --> 00:18:58,037
squared.

307
00:18:58,037 --> 00:19:01,250
The repeated expression is x squared.

308
00:19:01,250 --> 00:19:03,972
Let k equal x squared.

309
00:19:03,972 --> 00:19:08,883
The equation becomes k squared minus
thirteen k plus thirty-six

310
00:19:08,883 --> 00:19:10,899
equals zero.

311
00:19:10,899 --> 00:19:15,892
Factorise into k minus nine, multiplied
by k minus four,

312
00:19:15,892 --> 00:19:17,140
equals zero.

313
00:19:17,140 --> 00:19:20,353
Thus k equals nine or four.

314
00:19:20,353 --> 00:19:25,264
Return to x: x squared equals nine gives
x equals

315
00:19:25,264 --> 00:19:30,175
negative three or positive three. x
squared equals four gives

316
00:19:30,175 --> 00:19:32,897
negative two or positive two.

317
00:19:32,897 --> 00:19:36,877
We have four real solutions altogether.

318
00:19:36,877 --> 00:19:42,054
This example is a powerful contrast with
the exponential problem.

319
00:19:42,054 --> 00:19:46,966
If two to the power of x equals four,
then

320
00:19:46,966 --> 00:19:48,705
x equals two.

321
00:19:48,705 --> 00:19:53,616
But if x squared equals four, then x can
be

322
00:19:53,616 --> 00:19:56,338
negative two or positive two.

323
00:19:56,338 --> 00:20:02,283
The meaning of k determines how we undo
the substitution.

324
00:20:02,283 --> 00:20:04,513
Let's test the restriction.

325
00:20:04,513 --> 00:20:09,424
Suppose we have x to the fourth, plus
five x

326
00:20:09,424 --> 00:20:12,146
squared, plus four, equals zero.

327
00:20:12,146 --> 00:20:14,868
Let k equal x squared.

328
00:20:14,868 --> 00:20:19,779
We get k squared plus five k plus four
equals

329
00:20:19,779 --> 00:20:20,536
zero.

330
00:20:20,536 --> 00:20:25,447
Factorise into k plus one, times k plus
four, equals

331
00:20:25,447 --> 00:20:26,204
zero.

332
00:20:26,204 --> 00:20:31,167
So k equals negative one or negative
four.

333
00:20:31,167 --> 00:20:36,078
But k stands for x squared, and the
square of

334
00:20:36,078 --> 00:20:39,291
a real number cannot be negative.

335
00:20:39,291 --> 00:20:41,030
Reject both possibilities.

336
00:20:41,030 --> 00:20:45,010
Therefore there are no real solutions.

337
00:20:45,010 --> 00:20:49,922
It is important to say no real solutions,
not just

338
00:20:49,922 --> 00:20:51,170
no solutions.

339
00:20:51,170 --> 00:20:56,081
We are working in the real-number context
used for these

340
00:20:56,081 --> 00:20:58,588
Grade Twelve questions.

341
00:20:58,588 --> 00:21:02,783
Before our final practice, let's diagnose
three mistakes.

342
00:21:02,783 --> 00:21:07,694
Mistake one: writing two to the power of
two x

343
00:21:07,694 --> 00:21:12,871
as two multiplied by two to the power of
x.

344
00:21:12,871 --> 00:21:15,593
That breaks the exponent law.

345
00:21:15,593 --> 00:21:20,504
Mistake two: finding k and stopping, even
though the unknown

346
00:21:20,504 --> 00:21:21,753
is x.

347
00:21:21,753 --> 00:21:26,664
Mistake three: keeping an impossible k,
or rejecting a negative

348
00:21:26,664 --> 00:21:28,894
x that actually works.

349
00:21:28,894 --> 00:21:34,839
Those errors all come from forgetting
what the expression means.

350
00:21:34,839 --> 00:21:37,561
Here are two independent challenges.

351
00:21:37,561 --> 00:21:42,472
Please pause the video and solve them on
paper before

352
00:21:42,472 --> 00:21:45,470
I reveal the answers.

353
00:21:45,470 --> 00:21:50,381
Question one: nine to the power of x,
minus four

354
00:21:50,381 --> 00:21:55,292
times three to the power of x, plus
three, equals

355
00:21:55,292 --> 00:21:56,817
zero.

356
00:21:56,817 --> 00:22:01,810
Question two: x to the fourth, minus ten
x squared,

357
00:22:01,810 --> 00:22:04,808
plus nine, equals zero.

358
00:22:04,808 --> 00:22:08,021
Start by identifying the repeated
expression.

359
00:22:08,021 --> 00:22:12,932
Then do the substitution, solve, return,
and check which answers

360
00:22:12,932 --> 00:22:14,180
are allowed.

361
00:22:14,180 --> 00:22:17,669
Give yourself real thinking time.

362
00:22:17,669 --> 00:22:20,667
Ready for the solutions?

363
00:22:20,667 --> 00:22:25,578
For question one, nine to the power of x
is

364
00:22:25,578 --> 00:22:30,264
the square of three to the power of x.

365
00:22:30,264 --> 00:22:34,951
Let k equal three to the power of x.

366
00:22:34,951 --> 00:22:40,128
Then k squared minus four k plus three
equals zero.

367
00:22:40,128 --> 00:22:45,121
This factorises into k minus one, times k
minus three,

368
00:22:45,121 --> 00:22:46,369
equals zero.

369
00:22:46,369 --> 00:22:49,582
So k equals one or three.

370
00:22:49,582 --> 00:22:54,493
Going back gives three to the power of x
equals

371
00:22:54,493 --> 00:22:59,670
one, or three to the power of x equals
three.

372
00:22:59,670 --> 00:23:03,651
Therefore x equals zero or one.

373
00:23:03,651 --> 00:23:07,846
For question two, let k equal x squared.

374
00:23:07,846 --> 00:23:12,757
We get k squared minus ten k plus nine
equals

375
00:23:12,757 --> 00:23:13,514
zero.

376
00:23:13,514 --> 00:23:18,200
Factorise into k minus one, times k minus
nine.

377
00:23:18,200 --> 00:23:21,413
So k equals one or nine.

378
00:23:21,413 --> 00:23:26,324
This time, going back produces x equals
negative one, positive

379
00:23:26,324 --> 00:23:29,537
one, negative three or positive three.

380
00:23:29,537 --> 00:23:32,535
Four values in total.

381
00:23:32,535 --> 00:23:37,446
If you made a mistake, identify the stage
where it

382
00:23:37,446 --> 00:23:38,203
happened.

383
00:23:38,203 --> 00:23:41,416
Did you miss the repeated expression?

384
00:23:41,416 --> 00:23:45,120
Did you use an incorrect exponent law?

385
00:23:45,120 --> 00:23:47,350
Did you factorise incorrectly?

386
00:23:47,350 --> 00:23:51,545
Or did you forget to return to x?

387
00:23:51,545 --> 00:23:56,457
Practising that diagnosis is much more
useful than simply copying

388
00:23:56,457 --> 00:23:58,472
the solution.

389
00:23:58,472 --> 00:24:03,384
One final boundary to remember: not every
exponential equation has

390
00:24:03,384 --> 00:24:08,070
an answer that can be written using
whole-number powers.

391
00:24:08,070 --> 00:24:12,981
If two to the power of x equals seven,
matching

392
00:24:12,981 --> 00:24:16,194
simple integer exponents won't finish it.

393
00:24:16,194 --> 00:24:21,105
Logarithms can help in such cases, and we
treat those

394
00:24:21,105 --> 00:24:26,016
methods more fully with logarithmic
functions elsewhere in the CAPS

395
00:24:26,016 --> 00:24:26,773
course.

396
00:24:26,773 --> 00:24:31,685
For today's lesson, our focus has been
same-base equations and

397
00:24:31,685 --> 00:24:36,647
quadratic substitution, which you should
now recognise confidently.

398
00:24:36,647 --> 00:24:39,369
Now visit equationstationsa dot top.

399
